  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nMotrin / Percocet / Demerol\n\nChief Complaint:\nHospitalist Admission Note\nPatient Admitted on ___\nPt seen / examined at 1000\n.\nPrimary Oncologist:  Dr. ___\nPCP: Dr. ___ (___)\n.\nCC:  worsening ___ edema\n.\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nHPI:\n___ yo F with with metastatic carcinoma of unknown primary to \nbones (pathology suggestive of lobular breast carcinoma, ER \npositive) p/w subacutely worsening ___ edema, abdominal swelling \nand weight gain (25 lb's) over the last 2 - 3 weeks.  The ___ \nedema is associated with b/l ___ pain, limiting her ability to \nambulate, although she denies frank weakness.  She also endorses \nsome associated SOB / DOE, but denies frank chest pain.  She has \nhad some ___ edema chronically for months (> 6 months), had a \nbrief trial of PO Lasix at 20mg but self DC'ed as she felt it \nwas not improving.  She endorses mild dry cough, but denies \nfevers, chills.  She denies recent travel or sick contacts. No \nhistory of VTE.  On ROS, she notes urinary urgency and \nfrequency, but denies frank dysuria.  She also has chronic ___ \npain, which has been attributed to bony metastatic disease and \nis on chronic opioids.  She was actually seen by Pain Clinic \nyesterday for her lower extremity pain.  At that visit, they \nnoted her significant ___ edema, and sent her in to her outpt \noncology office for urgent visit.  She was then referred to ED.  \n\n.\nOther pertinent recent history includes hospitalization at ___ \n___ around ___, where per her report, she was dx'ed \nand treated for E. Coli bacteremia, likely due to liver abscess, \nwith a course of Ciprofloxacin.  \n.\nIn the ED, pt initially presented with stable VS, including \nnormal room air sat, but was placed on nasal cannula for \ncomfort.  She underwent CTA of Chest, CT abd/pelvis, which \nshowed no evidnece of PE, but did show some GGO's in b/l bases.  \nWas ordered for dose of Levaquin for CAP, however, shortly after \nreceiving dose, developed itching arm, so Levaquin DC'ed, then \nreceived Benadryl IV x 1 dose.  For pain received home morphine \ndose, Tylenol, Ultram 50mg, Dilaudid 1mg IV x1, Dilaudid 2mg PO \nx 1.     \n.\nOn arrival to the floor, the patient has no acute complaints.  \nShe just c/o feeling frustrated with the lack of a diagnosis and \ninability to resolve her edema. \n.\nReview of Systems: As per HPI. All other systems negative.\n.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ yo F with metastatic carcinoma of unknown primary, likely \nbreast, also PMH RAD / asthma on chronic prednisone, chronic ___ \nedema, admitted with worsening ___ edema, weight gain and SOB.\n\n# ___ edema:  \nCommon causes of edema were ruled out including CHF (normal \necho), liver failure, thyroid abnormalities, nephrotic syndrome, \nand thrombus. Possible causes include the aromatase inhibitor \nletrozole and/or chronic prednisone. She was initially diuresed \nwith lasix IV 40mg daily and then increased to BID with \nimprovement in her edema and dyspnea. She was then trialed on \noral Lasix and discharged on 80mg every morning for next ___ \ndays until ___ with PCP. Given its potential contribution \nto her edema it would be ideal to wean down her Prednisone, but \npt reports that she is dependent on Prednisone for her \nrespiratory status, and could not be weaned down to 5mg as \noutpatient in the past. Would also consider switching her \naromatase inhibitor, which her Oncologist can consider as an \noutpatient.\n\n# Hypokalemia:\nLikely from Lasix.  3.2 on day of discharge, repleted with 40mEq \nin AM and will take 40mEq in pm, then take 60mEq qAM until PCP \n___.  PCP should recheck potassium at f/u apopintment \n(patinet aware)\n\n# SOB:  \nPt reports that she has long standing history of asthma, is on \nchronic inhalers, as well as prednisone, which she frequently \nself-titrates.  No home O2.  She does not recall PFT's and there \nis no documentation of recent PFT's in the Atrius records.  She \nreports worsening SOB, but denies cough or fever.  CT scan did \nshow some multifocal GGO's, but she does not appear infected, so \nPNA was thought to be less likely.  No evidence of PE on her \nCTA. The question of potential lymphangitic spread of metastatic \ndisease was raised, but it was felt the risks of bronchoscopy \nwith biopsy outweighed the potential benefit. The patient was \nseen in consultation by Pulmonary (Dr. ___ and will follow \nclosely with them as an outpatient with full PFT's, an \noutpatient ABG, a sleep study, and a repeat CT scan in ___ \nweeks.  Patient has ___ appointment in early ___. \n\n# Metastatic Carcinoma:  \nPer review of outpatient records, its appears her tumor markers \nare stable. She was followed by ___ Oncology while an \ninpatient and will ___ with them outpatient for \nconsideration of changing her chemotherapy regimen should no \nalternative cause for her symptoms be found. \n\n# DM2, last A1C 7.5% (___): \nPt reports she is not on any medications or insulin as an \noutpatient.  \n\nTRANSITIONAL CARE ISSUES for PRIMARY CARE PHYSCIAN (office \ninformed)\n\n1. Recheck potassium ___\n2. Schedule outpatient PFTs, ABG (PCP should schedule)\n3. Schedule outpatient f/u CT chest ___ weeks (PCP should \nschedule)\n4. Patient informed of risk of sedation and respiratory \ndepression on concurrent opiate narcotic and benzodiazepine \nregimen.  I instructed her to not take in combination or in \nclose proximity, and not to drive or operate machinery on this. \nPatient states usually takes 1 lorazepam a day.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nLower extremity edema\nDyspnea\nMetastatic carcinoma of unknown primary\nMild Hypokalemia",
